    "sharedLink": "https://www.apeuni.com/practice/speaking?model=retell_lectures&num=640&menu=all",
    "promptText": "Listen to the lecture and retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.",
    "audioScript": "Today, I'm excited to shed light on the often overlooked yet vital role of microbes in our ecosystem and health. Microorganisms, though invisible to the naked eye, play an essential role in maintaining the balance of life on Earth. Microbes, including bacteria, viruses, and fungi, are everywhere - in soil, water, air, and even within our bodies. In the environment, they are crucial for processes like decomposition and nutrient cycling, essentially recycling life's essential elements. For instance, soil microbes break down organic matter, releasing nutrients that plants need to grow. In our bodies, microbes contribute significantly to our health. The human microbiome, the collection of all microorganisms living in and on our bodies, is essential for digestion, protecting against pathogens, and even regulating our immune system. Recent studies have shown that a diverse microbiome is linked to better health and can prevent certain diseases. In conclusion, microbes, despite their small size, have a colossal impact on our world and our health. Understanding and appreciating their role helps us realize the intricate connections between all forms of life.",
