    "imageUrl": null,
    "sharedLink": "https://www.apeuni.com/practice/speaking?model=retell_lectures&num=636&menu=all",
    "promptText": "Listen to the lecture and retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.",
    "audioScript": "In this presentation, I'm excited to delve into the revolutionary role of antibiotics in modern medicine. The discovery of antibiotics marked a turning point in medical history, drastically changing the way we treat infections. Before the advent of antibiotics, simple infections could be deadly, and surgeries were high-risk procedures. The introduction of penicillin in the 1940s, discovered by Alexander Fleming, changed everything. It became the first widely used antibiotic, turning once-fatal diseases into treatable conditions. Antibiotics have not only saved countless lives but also have played a critical role in advancing medical procedures. They made complex surgeries safer and organ transplants possible by preventing and treating bacterial infections. However, it's crucial to address the growing concern of antibiotic resistance due to overuse and misuse. This highlights the importance of responsible antibiotic use and ongoing research for new treatments. In conclusion, antibiotics are a cornerstone of modern medicine, dramatically improving our ability to fight infections. Their discovery and development remind us of the incredible impact of medical research and innovation.",
