    "promptText": "Do you agree that people are too dependent on technology?",
    "referenceAnswer": "In the midst of the digital revolution, there is a prevailing notion that people have become excessively reliant on technology for various aspects of their lives. However, a closer examination reveals that while technology plays a significant role in modern society, individuals are not as dependent on it as commonly perceived. This essay explores the positive standpoint that people are not overwhelmingly reliant on technology and highlights the resilience and adaptability of individuals in navigating the digital landscape. \nContrary to the belief that technology dependency is ubiquitous, many individuals exhibit a balanced approach to technology use. While technology enhances convenience and efficiency, people demonstrate the ability to disconnect and engage in offline activities. Whether through mindfulness practices, hobbies, or outdoor pursuits, individuals actively seek moments of unplugged time to rejuvenate and connect with the world beyond screens. This intentional balance showcases a conscious effort to maintain autonomy and control over technology use. \nFurthermore, human ingenuity and creativity remain at the forefront of innovation, shaping technology to suit individual needs and preferences. Rather than being passive consumers of technology, individuals actively participate in its development and evolution. The diverse ways in which people customize technology to enhance productivity, creativity, and personal growth underscore the dynamic relationship between humans and technology. This proactive engagement demonstrates that while technology is a valuable tool, it does not dictate or dominate every aspect of human life. \nIn conclusion, the narrative of overwhelming dependency on technology overlooks the agency and adaptability of individuals in managing their relationship with digital tools. While technology has undoubtedly transformed society, people demonstrate a capacity to maintain autonomy and balance in their interactions with technology.",
